+*RESPONSES FROM EY (5 SEPT)*+

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
*Bolt Q*: “_our Privacy team would like to understand if EY is able to sign a Data Sharing Agreement with Bolt Services RO (attached draft)_”
{panel}

*EY Response*: “_The EY General Terms and Conditions (part of the agreement provided) already contain the clauses regarding data processing by EY as an operator, therefore we do not have to conclude the attached document_”

*Privacy Thoughts*: The EY General T&Cs of 13 June 2024 (attached to parent ticket), in particular Appendix 1, contain data protection provisions at Clauses 21-23. The main points from these clauses are:

* EY will process any personal data provided by Bolt in accordance with applicable laws.
* EY will process the data as an independent controller.

EY is correct that it is not _obliged_ to enter into a DSA, as unlike a controller-processor relationship, it is not mandatory to ensure that this data sharing arrangement is covered by an agreement. However, this does not change the fact that we ordinarily push for DSAs to protect Bolt’s position - for example, EY’s General T&C do not contain a pro-Bolt indemnity.

In short - we should still try and push back here. It is a business decision whether the business is willing to accept not implementing a DSA, but we should try explaining the position to EY first.

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
*Bolt Q*: “_Under this proposed arrangement, will GAC be processing Bolt employee data on EY’s behalf as a processor, or as an independent controller?_”
{panel}

*EY Response*: “_As an independent controller_”

*Privacy Thoughts*: This mirrors the relationship set out in EY’s T&Cs. No additional comments.

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
*Bolt Q*: “_Are there any data processing / sharing agreements currently in place between EY and GAC that could cover GAC’s processing of Bolt employee data under this arrangement?_”
{panel}

*EY Response*: “_Yes, the terms of our subcontracting agreement with GAC include clauses regarding the processing of personal data._”

*Privacy Thoughts*: EY has not provided this subcontracting agreement. IMO this makes it even more important to enter into a DSA, so that we can ensure that the data is properly protected.

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
*Bolt Q*: “_Will GAC be sharing Bolt employee data with any additional third parties?_”
{panel}

*EY Response*: “_There is no third party involved and GAC does not share any information. During the certification process, there are specific forms that need to be filled that will contain information regarding Bolt employees involved in R&D activities in order be reviewed by the technical expert appointed from the Ministry of Research. This information is part of the certification file prepared by both EY&GAC, required by law in order to obtain the certificate of expertise. During this process Bolt is in direct dialogue with the expert and will share all the information required by law with him and EY_”

*Privacy Thoughts*: This is not unsurprising. However, again, since EY will be sharing the employee data with the MoR on Bolt’s behalf, IMO this strengthens the case for entering into a DSA to protect Bolt’s position.

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
*Bolt Q*: “_What is the full list of categories of personal data that will be shared by Bolt with EY (and subsequently, GAC) under this arrangement?_”
{panel}

*EY Response*: “_For the feasibility analysis, in order to organize the interviews, we need the e-mail addresses/phone numbers of the team leaders and technical/project managers involved in R&D activities. In order to fill point 5. from the order of expertise - Annex 3.3 - Opis with the main documents annexed to the expertise order - Bolt must provide the list of staff involved in the R&D activities for the R&D project subject to certification and this list must also include staff name & surname, function in the project, last studies, diploma obtained, name of the educational unit. Also, to fill point 17 from the order of expertise - Annex 3.3 - Bolt must provide the information on the categories of expenditure for each member of staff involved in the R&D activities subject to certification, and this list must also include staff name, surname, function, gross salary, meal vouchers, bonuses, allowances, private pensions, per diems_.”

*Privacy Thoughts*: In summary, the personal data to be shared is employee email addresses, phone numbers, full names, role, details of diploma, gross salary, meal vouchers, bonuses, allowances, private pensions and per diems. In light of the arrangement proposed, none of this data appears to be disproportionate. I will update the DSA accordingly.

